// ── 类型 ──
SDL_PowerState :: i32

// ── 常量 ──
//  Plugged in, battery charged
SDL_POWERSTATE_CHARGED :: 4
//  Plugged in, charging battery
SDL_POWERSTATE_CHARGING :: 3
//  error determining power status
SDL_POWERSTATE_ERROR :: -1
//  Plugged in, no battery available
SDL_POWERSTATE_NO_BATTERY :: 2
//  Not plugged in, running on the battery
SDL_POWERSTATE_ON_BATTERY :: 1
//  cannot determine power status
SDL_POWERSTATE_UNKNOWN :: 0

// ── 函数 ──
//  Get the current power supply details. You should never take a battery status as absolute truth.
//   Batteries (especially failing batteries) are delicate hardware, and the values reported here
//   are best estimates based on what that hardware reports. It's not uncommon for older batteries
//   to lose stored power much faster than it reports, or completely drain when reporting it has 20
//   percent left, etc. Battery status can change at any time; if you are concerned with power
//   state, you should call this function frequently, and perhaps ignore changes until they seem to
//   be stable for a few seconds. It's possible a platform can only report battery percentage or
//   time left but not both. On some platforms, retrieving power supply details might be expensive.
//   If you want to display continuous status you could call this function every minute or so.
//
//  This function is not thread safe.
//
//  \param seconds — a pointer filled in with the seconds of battery life left, or NULL to ignore.
//   This will be filled in with -1 if we can't determine a value or there is no battery.
//  \param percent — a pointer filled in with the percentage of battery life left, between 0 and
//   100, or NULL to ignore. This will be filled in with -1 when we can't determine a value or there
//   is no battery.
//
//  \returns the current battery state or `SDL_POWERSTATE_ERROR` on failure; call SDL_GetError() for
//   more information.
//
//  \since This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
SDL_GetPowerInfo :: (seconds: *i32, percent: *i32) -> SDL_PowerState extern_C
